.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Interface for an object which can be serialized\&. 


.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
\fBAPI Stability: 3 — Stable\fP\&. This public contract is established\&. Compatible changes are expected; intentional breaking changes require an announced breaking release and migration guidance\&. See \fBAPI stability\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
API stability does not promise a fixed snapshot byte format; see \fBAPI stability\fP\&.

.PP
Declare a JavaScript class extending \fR\fBpdg\&.ISerializable\fP\fP and implement these four methods:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetSerializedSize(serializer)\fP returns the byte count for the object's data\&. Use the serializer's \fRsizeof_*()\fP methods to match its encoding\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRserialize(serializer)\fP writes the object's fields using \fRserialize_*()\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdeserialize(deserializer)\fP reads them using \fRdeserialize_*()\fP, in the same order and with the same types used when writing\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetMyClassTag()\fP returns a stable, unique unsigned 32-bit tag identifying the class\&. Zero is invalid; \fR0xFFFFFF00\fP through \fR0xFFFFFFFF\fP are reserved by PDG\&.
.PP

.PP
The native constructor takes four callbacks in that order\&. Pass forwarding functions to \fRsuper()\fP so native serialization calls your class methods with the correct receiver\&. Initialize instance fields after \fRsuper()\fP returns\&.

.PP
Register the class with \fR\fBpdg\&.registerSerializableClass()\fP\fP before deserializing its objects\&. The registered constructor must support being called without arguments, so provide defaults for any constructor parameters\&.
.SS "Example"
.PP
.nf
class MySerializableClass extends pdg\&.ISerializable {
    constructor(one = 15, two = 99) {
        super(
            function(serializer) { return this\&.getSerializedSize(serializer); },
            function(serializer) { this\&.serialize(serializer); },
            function(deserializer) { this\&.deserialize(deserializer); },
            function() { return this\&.getMyClassTag(); }
        );
        this\&.one = one;
        this\&.two = two;
    }

    getSerializedSize(serializer) {
        return serializer\&.sizeof_1u(this\&.one) + serializer\&.sizeof_2u(this\&.two);
    }

    serialize(serializer) {
        serializer\&.serialize_1u(this\&.one);
        serializer\&.serialize_2u(this\&.two);
    }

    deserialize(deserializer) {
        this\&.one = deserializer\&.deserialize_1u();
        this\&.two = deserializer\&.deserialize_2u();
    }

    getMyClassTag() {
        return 0x4068; // reserve a unique tag for this class in your application
    }
}

pdg\&.registerSerializableClass(MySerializableClass);
const original = new MySerializableClass();

const serializer = new pdg\&.Serializer();
serializer\&.serialize_obj(original);
serializer\&.serialize_obj(original); // writes a reference to the same object

const deserializer = new pdg\&.Deserializer();
deserializer\&.setDataPtr(serializer\&.getDataPtr());
const first = deserializer\&.deserialize_obj();
const second = deserializer\&.deserialize_obj();

console\&.assert(first instanceof MySerializableClass);
console\&.assert(first\&.one === 15 && first\&.two === 99);
console\&.assert(second === first); // shared references preserve object identity
.fi
.PP

.PP
Objects can serialize other objects with \fRserialize_obj()\fP\&. The serializer tracks objects within the stream, so repeated references and parent/child cycles do not require duplicate object data\&. Use \fRsizeof_obj()\fP when calculating space for nested serialized objects\&.

.PP
